We start by asking, "what can we multiply by $x-5$ to get $x^2-9x+k$ where $k$ is an integer?"

The answer is $x-4$:

$\blueD{(x-5)(x-4)=x^2-9x+20}$

Now we rewrite the numerator and split the quotient:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{x^2-9x+14}{x-5}&=\dfrac{(\blueD{x^2-9x+20})-6}{x-5}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD{x^2-9x+20}}{x-5}-\dfrac{6}{x-5}
\end{align}$

Now we use the fact that $\blueD{(x-5)(x-4)=x^2-9x+20}$ to rewrite the numerator of the first quotient and cancel common factors:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{\blueD{x^2-9x+20}}{x-5}-\dfrac{6}{x-5}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{\blueD{(x-5)}}\blueD{(x-4)}}{\cancel{x-5}}-\dfrac{6}{x-5}
\\\\
&=x-4-\dfrac{6}{x-5}
\end{align}$
